  "rationale": "**Verdict:** `requirements.index.chapter` is implemented and tested, and I found no unmarked code or unspecified behavior.\n\n**Implementation, all marked `{impl}` in `src/index.rs`:**\n- **Directive detection (`marker_ranges`):**\n  - It finds a standalone `{requirements-index}` colon-fence line.\n  - It closes the directive on a later colon fence with at least as many colons and nothing else on the line.\n  - It rejects a missing closing fence and a non-empty body, both through `invalid_index`.\n  - It skips fenced code blocks and other protected ranges, and it skips directives nested inside definition blocks, so examples are ignored.\n- **At most one directive per book (`DetailPlan::find`):** it scans every chapter's ranges and returns an error on a second directive.\n- **Replacement (`DetailPlan::populate`):**\n  - It builds one table row per detail page. Each row links to the detail page through `chapter_href`, which `lib.rs` marks `{impl}` for `requirements.index.navigation`.\n  - It replaces the directive's range in the index chapter with the table from `render_evidence_table`.\n  - It errors if the index chapter has disappeared.\n- **Detail pages:** `DetailPlan::find` and `populate` are marked `{impl}` for `requirements.index.detail_pages`, as is `preprocess_book` in `src/lib.rs`. The pages are nested subchapters under the defining chapter, in definition order, titled by full name, at `detail_path`.\n- **Helpers and extra behavior:**\n  - The helpers `colon_fence`, `protected_ranges` and `definition_blocks` in `src/lib.rs` carry their own `{impl}` markers for related requirements.\n  - The extras in the table (the state column and example rows) are covered by other requirements that the code marks: `requirements.index.state` and `requirements.examples.index`.\n\n**Tests, all marked `{test}`:**\n- `marker_accepts_long_fences_and_ignores_examples` covers four-colon fences, a longer closing fence (five colons closing four), and directives inside a code block or a `req` example, which are ignored. It also covers a missing closing fence and a non-empty body, which are errors.\n- `index_shows_evidence_states_and_links_generated_chapters` runs the whole pipeline. The directive is replaced, each requirement gets a row linking to its detail page, and the table is aligned. The detail pages appear as subchapters in order, with the expected path, number and name.\n- `repeated_index_markers_fail_with_a_clear_error` covers the \"no more than one\" rule across two chapters.\n\n**Residual gaps:** I did not see tests for indented directives or for a closing fence shorter than the opening one. These are minor edge cases, and the logic for them is simple and visible in `marker_ranges` and `colon_fence`.",
